"\n\n\n\n\n## Review\nA tour we booked from the start of planning our Australia trip, determined to see a quokka no matter what.  \n\nTouching wild quokkas is illegal, but at this wildlife park you can feed and pet them directly.  \nSince quokkas and many other animals are nocturnal, most tours depart early in the morning to arrive right at opening time.  \nWe gathered early in the morning and headed to the park with our guide.  \n\nListening to our guide's stories about Australian history along the way, we arrived at the park without a moment of boredom and received our entry tickets and a map of the grounds.  \n\nThe park isn't large, so there was plenty of time, but wanting to see the quokkas before they dozed off, we rushed straight to the quokka enclosure at the back as soon as we entered.  \nIt was our first time seeing one in person, and just as expected, they were absolutely adorable.  \n\nWhen we quickly offered some food, the quokka grabbed my hand and ate from it — and the way it ate was incredibly cute.  \nI gently petted it between bites and it didn't seem to mind at all.  \nThe fur was much softer than expected.  \n\nSince we were the first ones to rush to the quokka area, we had them almost to ourselves for a few minutes.  \nNo matter how long we watched, they stayed cute, so we spent a long time feeding and petting them.  \n\nOnce the other people in our tour group made their way to the quokkas, we reluctantly gave up our spot and made our way back to leisurely look at the animals we had passed by.  \n\nWallabies were wandering around freely, and they ate eagerly when we offered food.  \nWe took photos with them and petted them — they were much more docile than expected.  \n\nWe also got to see dingoes and koalas, and got up close with a wide variety of native wildlife.  \n\nAs more tourists arrived, the animals got fuller, and they stopped approaching even when offered food — so we ended up being the ones begging them to eat.  \n\nThese animals alone would make a trip to Australia completely worth it.  \n## Pictures\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n"